Chapter: MathLib

 We say:

Section: Definitions

 int-widen answers its argument unchanged and exists for its TYPE. A type
 argument is invariant (`docs/DevelopersGuide.md` "Variance of Type
 Arguments"), so a list literal or tuple built from a bounded field carries
 that field's bounds and cannot stand where a plain `List Integer` or a
 plain `Integer` component is wanted. Widening at an ordinary parameter and
 return is still allowed, and with no cast and no type ascription in the
 language this is the only way to spell it.

  int-widen : Integer -> Integer
  int-widen (n) = n

  square : Integer -> Integer
  square (x) = x * x

  cube : Integer -> Integer
  cube (x) = x * x * x

  helper : Integer -> Integer
  helper (x) = x + 1

Section: Common Helpers
  math-abs : Integer -> Integer
  math-abs (n) = if n < 0 then -n else n

  math-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  math-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  math-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  math-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  math-clamp : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  math-clamp (lo) (hi) (v) = if v < lo then lo else if v > hi then hi else v

  math-mod : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  math-mod (a) (b) = a - (a / b) * b

  math-isqrt : Integer -> Integer
  math-isqrt (n) = if n <= 0 then 0 else math-isqrt-loop n (n / 2 + 1)

  math-isqrt-loop : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  math-isqrt-loop (n) (guess) =
    let next = (guess + n / guess) / 2
    in if next >= guess then guess else math-isqrt-loop n next

